---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import SiteLayout from "../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
import shortageImage from "../../assets/ideas/the-shortage-that-cannot-hide.png";
import { pageSeo } from "../../seo/registry.mjs";
---

<SiteLayout metadata={pageSeo["/ideas/the-shortage-that-cannot-hide"]}>
	<main id="main" class="ideas-post">
		<article aria-labelledby="post-title">
			<header class="opening reading">
				<p class="eyebrow">3Back point of view</p>
				<h1 id="post-title">The Shortage That Cannot Hide</h1>
				<p class="lead">What the skilled trades reveal about domain capability in the rest of work</p>
			</header>

			<figure class="post-figure reading">
				<Image
					src={shortageImage}
					alt="A technician kneels at a server rack and seats an orange cable under a dense overhead run of labeled cables, including a tag that reads DO NOT UNPLUG."
					widths={[480, 760, 1200]}
					sizes="(max-width: 808px) calc(100vw - 48px), 760px"
					loading="eager"
				/>
			</figure>

			<section class="reading prose" aria-label="The shortage that cannot hide">
				<p>America has a skilled-labor problem.</p>
				<p>A new report from the Alliance for America's Skilled Trades estimates that the United States will need to fill roughly 1.7 million skilled-trades openings each year through 2035. Current training programs are producing only about 55 workers for every 100 needed.</p>
				<p>The immediate problem is easy to see.</p>
				<p>Someone has to build the data centers, maintain the machinery, wire the electrical systems, install the cooling, repair the vehicles, and keep the physical infrastructure underneath the economy running.</p>
				<p>But there is a larger problem hiding inside the skilled-trades shortage.</p>
				<blockquote>This may be the part of the problem that cannot hide.</blockquote>
			</section>

			<section class="reading prose">
				<h2>The work eventually tells you</h2>
				<p>Skilled trades have an unforgiving characteristic: eventually, somebody has to do the work.</p>
				<p>The electrician has to wire the panel. The machinist has to make the part. The mechanic has to diagnose the failure. The carpenter has to build something that fits, carries the load, and stays standing.</p>
				<p>Education matters in all of those fields. Technical knowledge matters. Training matters. But none of them can permanently substitute for the ability to perform against actual conditions.</p>
				<blockquote>The domain eventually answers back.</blockquote>
				<p>A person who knows the terminology but cannot do the work becomes visible. A training program that produces graduates without sufficient capability eventually becomes visible. An organization that lacks enough people capable of performing the work eventually feels the consequence directly.</p>
				<p>In the trades, consequence arrives on a short clock. The work itself provides a bench, a place where capability has to become observable.</p>
				<p>That distinction becomes much easier to conceal when the work moves away from the physical world.</p>
			</section>

			<section class="reading prose">
				<h2>Most knowledge work never gets a bench</h2>
				<p>Cause and effect in knowledge work can be separated by weeks, months, or years. Responsibility can be distributed across teams, managers, vendors, systems, and functions. A poor decision can be absorbed by the organization long before anyone can identify the judgment that produced it.</p>
				<p>There are several common ways that missing capability can remain hidden.</p>
				<p>Fluency without understanding. Someone can know the vocabulary of product development without understanding the product, the customer, or the market.</p>
				<p>Method knowledge without work knowledge. Someone can understand a management framework while knowing very little about the work of the people expected to use it.</p>
				<p>Authority ahead of judgment. Someone can earn a credential, satisfy a hiring requirement, and gain authority to apply a method before experience has taught them when it fits, when it does not, or what its application is doing to the work.</p>
				<p>All three can look like preparation. None necessarily demonstrates professional capability.</p>
				<blockquote>In the trades, missing domain capability tends to become visible. In knowledge work, an organization can carry it for a long time.</blockquote>
				<p>The bench can look full while the capability is thin.</p>
			</section>

			<section class="reading prose">
				<h2>We became very good at producing marks</h2>
				<p>This is not an argument against universities.</p>
				<p>Education can establish knowledge, expose people to ideas they would not otherwise encounter, develop analytical ability, and prepare someone to enter a profession.</p>
				<p>The problem begins when we ask evidence of education to prove something it cannot prove by itself.</p>
				<p>Harvard Business School research on degree inflation documented employers adding degree requirements to jobs that historically had not required them, often using the degree as a proxy for qualities employers wanted but were not directly observing.</p>
				<blockquote>Completion becomes qualification.<br />Qualification becomes capability.<br />Capability becomes authority.</blockquote>
				<p>Each transition can be justified. None is automatic. The mistake is allowing the mark to end the inquiry into capability.</p>
				<p>A sanitized learning environment can isolate a concept and make it easier to understand. Real work does the opposite. It introduces conflicting objectives, incomplete information, customers, history, technical debt, budgets, regulation, personalities, deadlines, and consequences.</p>
				<p>Those conditions are not interference surrounding professional capability. They are where professional capability develops.</p>
				<blockquote>The trades did not invent the distinction between education and professional capability. They simply operate in environments where the distinction is difficult to conceal.</blockquote>
			</section>

			<section class="reading prose">
				<h2>The cost can be worse than a shortage</h2>
				<p>A shortage tells you that capability is missing. A hidden capability deficit can change the organization before anyone realizes what is happening.</p>
				<p>Consider a compounding-pharmacy business beginning to scale.</p>
				<p>At the compounding bench, the domain is difficult to fake. Formulations, concentrations, procedures, controls, and consequences are real. The work provides evidence.</p>
				<p>Now move one layer away from the bench and add an infusion of capital with an expectation of rapid growth. The company hires. Management layers appear. New systems are commissioned. Workflows are standardized. Software is added. Procedures are formalized so that more people can be brought into the organization faster.</p>
				<p>Much of that is necessary.</p>
				<p>But suppose a question that once got resolved by an experienced pharmacist walking to the bench is encoded into the new system: a required field, routed to a queue, followed by an approval step. On paper it looks cleaner. It creates a record and appears more controlled.</p>
				<p>It goes live. Nothing breaks.</p>
				<p>Then the exceptions start. The people closest to the work create workarounds. One approval becomes two because the first no longer carries enough context. A dashboard appears to track the queue. Each addition solves the problem immediately in front of it, and each becomes another dependency the next decision has to respect.</p>
				<p>The original capability problem has now become structural.</p>
				<blockquote>The most dangerous work is not always work that fails. It is work that survives long enough to become infrastructure.</blockquote>
				<p>A data center can still be running while nobody at the rack can see what an apparently local cable change will affect. The hall still runs. The dependency is no longer local.</p>
				<p>This is where capital can become an accelerant. Investors put capital into a company because they expect the business to grow and the return on that capital to compound. But capital can allow headcount, systems, process, and authority to expand much faster than domain judgment can develop.</p>
				<blockquote>Capital is supposed to compound returns. Premature scaling can compound the mess instead.</blockquote>
				<p>Research on startup scaling describes the hazard as commitment risk: resources are committed before enough learning has occurred.</p>
				<p>The point is not that investment causes poor execution. It is that capital can remove a constraint that once forced the organization to learn before it multiplied.</p>
				<blockquote>You can fund growth much faster than you can develop judgment.</blockquote>
			</section>

			<section class="reading prose">
				<h2>Then the organization can begin hiding the evidence from itself</h2>
				<p>Knowledge work has another concealment mechanism: the evidence has to travel through an organization.</p>
				<p>Once someone has been hired, promoted, or given authority on the assumption that they are capable, contrary evidence becomes uncomfortable. It reflects not only on the person doing the work, but potentially on the managers who selected them, promoted them, or built a function around them.</p>
				<p>That does not require deliberate deception. Organizations provide plenty of more comfortable explanations: the team needs more support, the process needs refinement, another department created the dependency, the organization needs better alignment, the work is unusually complex, the initiative needs more time.</p>
				<p>Any one of those explanations may be true. They can also sanitize the underlying signal.</p>
				<p>What began as insufficient judgment close to the work can arrive at senior leadership as an alignment problem, a transformation problem, a communication problem, or simply the unavoidable complexity of the business.</p>
				<p>By then, the original question may have disappeared:</p>
				<blockquote>Do the people making these decisions actually possess enough expertise in this domain to make them well?</blockquote>
				<p>The delay does not merely postpone recognition.</p>
				<blockquote>It gives the damage time to compound.</blockquote>
			</section>

			<section class="reading prose">
				<h2>Now add AI</h2>
				<p>AI did not create this problem. It raises the cost of getting selection wrong.</p>
				<p>A person with limited domain expertise can now produce credible-looking analysis, code, plans, documentation, recommendations, process designs, and architectural alternatives at a level and volume that previously required far more accumulated experience.</p>
				<p>Production becomes cheaper. Selection does not.</p>
				<p>Every generated artifact still requires someone to decide what belongs, what should be discarded, which assumption is dangerous, and what the system can safely absorb. If that judgment is weak, AI can add code, process, structure, and dependencies faster than the organization can learn what they are doing.</p>
				<blockquote>The problem is no longer simply whether someone can produce the work. It is whether they have enough domain capability to govern what gets produced.</blockquote>
			</section>

			<section class="reading prose">
				<h2>Capability develops in contact with consequence</h2>
				<p>None of this means organizations should abandon formal education.</p>
				<p>Nor does every profession need to reproduce a traditional trade apprenticeship.</p>
				<p>But apprenticeship preserves something that organizations routinely say they value and then abandon under pressure: a sequence in which responsibility grows as capability becomes visible.</p>
				<ol>
					<li>Build foundational knowledge. Give people the concepts and understanding needed to enter the work intelligently.</li>
					<li>Put that knowledge in contact with real conditions. Let people work against actual demand, constraints, customers, systems, and consequences alongside people with deeper experience.</li>
					<li>Make judgment observable. Let them make decisions, see the effects, compare expectation with reality, and correct their interpretation.</li>
					<li>Expand authority as capability becomes evident. Responsibility grows because judgment has been demonstrated in the work, not simply because another program was completed.</li>
				</ol>
				<p>The point is not that apprenticeship is the universal answer. The point is the sequence.</p>
				<p>Organizations often understand that sequence perfectly well in theory. Then capital arrives, growth targets rise, and the sequence gets compressed. Hiring outruns development. Authority outruns evidence. The organization needs people now, so the best available proxy becomes proof and the organization keeps moving.</p>
				<p>That can accelerate growth. It can also accelerate everything the organization will eventually have to untangle.</p>
			</section>

			<section class="reading prose">
				<h2>The question for leaders</h2>
				<p>The skilled-trades shortage deserves attention on its own. Executives should also pay attention to what it reveals.</p>
				<p>The trades operate close enough to consequence that weak capability becomes difficult to conceal. Knowledge-work organizations have more room to avoid that evidence. They can mistake fluency for judgment, credentials for capability, activity for progress, and complexity for sophistication. Weak decisions can become infrastructure while the evidence of those decisions is cleaned up as it moves through the organization.</p>
				<p>For a while, everything may look fine.</p>
				<p>A leader does not need a new program to start looking. Consider an automotive service operation. Most of us know cars well enough to feel familiar with the domain. Then a vehicle arrives because the driver's seat no longer behaves correctly. The seat motor works. The switches work. The problem may begin somewhere else entirely. A Bluetooth-based phone key identifies the driver, software associates that identity with a saved profile, other electronic modules communicate that state across the vehicle network, and the seat controller acts on it. A failure somewhere in that chain can show up at the seat even though nothing is wrong with the seat itself.</p>
				<p>Someone familiar with dealerships and automobiles can look at that problem and feel reasonably confident about what needs fixing. Domain expertise changes the question: Which dependency actually produced the behavior, and what else moves if we change it?</p>
				<p>The more important question is not simply whether the organization has enough people.</p>
				<p>It is:</p>
				<blockquote>Where are we relying on evidence that someone was prepared instead of evidence that they can perform?</blockquote>
				<blockquote>Where the work will not answer that question quickly, leadership has to go looking for the evidence.</blockquote>
			</section>

			<section class="reading prose sources" aria-labelledby="sources-title">
				<h2 id="sources-title">Sources</h2>
				<ol>
					<li>Alliance for America's Skilled Trades. The State of America's Skilled Trades: A National Report (2026). The Alliance reports roughly 1.7 million skilled-trades openings per year through 2035 and 55 workers in training for every 100 needed. <a href="https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20260930290396/en/">Business Wire source release</a>.</li>
					<li>Harvard Business School, Accenture, and Grads of Life. Dismissed by Degrees: How degree inflation is undermining U.S. competitiveness and hurting America's middle class (2017). <a href="https://www.hbs.edu/faculty/Pages/item.aspx?num=53502">Report</a>.</li>
					<li>Ando, Yoshiki; Dinlersoz, Emin; Greenwood, Jeremy; and Piazzesi, Ruben. Technifying Ventures. NBER Working Paper 33993, revised March 2026. The paper reports large employment gains after VC funding among a concentrated group of firms. <a href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w33993">NBER</a>.</li>
					<li>Lee, Saerom (Ronnie), and J. Daniel Kim. When do startups scale? Large-scale evidence from job postings. Strategic Management Journal 45(9), 2024. The study associates early scaling with higher failure rates and identifies commitment risk as a central mechanism. <a href="https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/smj.3596">Wiley</a>.</li>
					<li>U.S. Department of Labor, Chief Evaluation Office. The Impact of Registered and Unregistered Apprenticeship: Evidence from the Scaling Apprenticeship and Closing the Skills Gap Grants (released April 30, 2026). <a href="https://www.dol.gov/resource-library/impact-registered-and-unregistered-apprenticeship-evidence-scaling-apprenticeship">DOL evaluation</a>.</li>
				</ol>
			</section>

			<footer class="reading post-close">
